{title:Title}

{pstd}{bf:cdistinct} {hline 2} Report distinct values of variables

{marker syntax}{...}
{title:Syntax}

{p 8 12 2}{cmd:cdistinct} [{varlist}] {ifin}
[{cmd:,} {opt miss:ing} {opt a:bbrev(#)} {opt j:oint}
{opt min:imum(#)} {opt max:imum(#)} {opt sort(specification)}
{opt w:idth(#)} {opt verbose} {opt threads(#)}]

{pstd}{cmd:by:} is allowed. {it:specification} is one of {opt a:lpha},
{opt di:stinct}, or {opt t:otal}, optionally with {opt de:scending}.

{marker description}{...}
{title:Description}

{* ctools-credit:begin (generated by scripts/sync_attribution.py; edit config/attribution.json)}{...}
{pstd}
{cmd:cdistinct} reimplements {cmd:distinct} 1.4.3 (September 3, 2023) by Gary
Longton and Nicholas J. Cox. See
{help cdistinct##acknowledgments:Acknowledgments} for details.
{* ctools-credit:end}{...}

{pstd}{cmd:cdistinct} reports the observation count and distinct-value count for
each variable, with the syntax, table, messages, and stored results of
{cmd:distinct} by Gary Longton and Nicholas J. Cox. Omitting the
variable list selects all variables. Numeric and string variables are allowed.
Missing values are excluded separately for each variable unless {opt missing}
is specified. The command leaves values and observation order unchanged.

{pstd}{cmd:cdistinct} counts distinct values exactly, {cmd:strL} values of any
length and binary ones included: values the plugin cannot read (over 2,045
bytes, or binary) are counted through their ranks in Stata's sort order.

{marker options}{...}
{title:Options}

{pstd}{opt missing} includes numeric missing values and empty strings.
Extended numeric missing codes count separately.

{pstd}{opt abbrev(#)} displays variable names abbreviated to at most {it:#}
characters. The default is 32, which shows every name in full; values below 5
are treated as 5. This option has no effect with {opt joint}.

{pstd}{opt joint} counts combinations of the entire variable list and reports
them in one row, labeled {cmd:(jointly)}. Without {opt missing}, an
observation is excluded if any variable in the list is missing. If no
observation is left to count, {opt joint} stops with error r(2000).

{pstd}{opt minimum(#)} and {opt maximum(#)} display only the rows whose number
of distinct values is at least, or at most, {it:#}. The defaults are 1 and no
upper bound, so a variable with no nonmissing values is not shown unless
{cmd:minimum(0)} is specified. Reversed limits are exchanged, with a message.
If no row is left, a note is displayed instead of the table.

{pstd}{opt sort(specification)} displays the rows sorted. {it:specification}
is one of {opt a:lpha}, {opt di:stinct}, or {opt t:otal}, to which
{opt de:scending} may be added. This option has no effect with {opt joint}.

{phang2}{cmd:alpha} sorts by variable name as displayed, in the order A before
Z before _ before a before z.{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:distinct} sorts by the number of distinct values.{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:total} sorts by the number of observations counted.{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:descending} reverses the order.{p_end}

{pstd}Each word may be abbreviated; {cmd:d} alone is refused because it could
mean {cmd:distinct} or {cmd:descending}. Rows that tie stay in the order of
{varlist}. {opt sort} without an argument means {cmd:sort(distinct)}.

{pstd}{opt width(#)} sets the width of each of the two columns of counts. The
smallest width, and the default, is 11; above ten million observations the
default grows with the number of observations. {opt width()} and {opt sort}
without an argument are accepted by {cmd:distinct} but are not in its help
file.

{pstd}{opt threads(#)} sets the maximum number of threads, from 1 to 256. Zero
or omission uses the {help ctools##threads:ctools setting}.

{pstd}{opt verbose} reports load and count times above the table.

{marker examples}{...}
{title:Examples}

{phang2}{cmd:. cdistinct firm year}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cdistinct firm year, joint missing}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cdistinct, sort(distinct descending)}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cdistinct, maximum(10) sort(alpha)}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. bysort industry: cdistinct firm}{p_end}

{marker results}{...}
{title:Stored results}

{pstd}{cmd:r(N)} is the number of observations counted and {cmd:r(ndistinct)}
is the number of distinct values, for the last variable in {varlist} or for
the variables considered jointly and, with {cmd:by:}, for the last group. They
describe the last variable whether or not {opt minimum()} and {opt maximum()}
let its row be shown. Both are 0 when no row at all is shown, which includes
the case in which no observation satisfies {cmd:if} and {cmd:in}.

{title:Remarks}

{pstd}{cmd:cdistinct} follows {cmd:distinct} 1.4.3. SSC distributes an older
version, 1.2.1, with a different table and different defaults; see
{help cdistinct##acknowledgments:Acknowledgments}. {opt threads()} and
{opt verbose} are additions in ctools. {cmd:cdistinct} differs from
{cmd:distinct} 1.4.3 in the following ways.

{pstd}{cmd:in} selects the same original rows for every variable. In
{cmd:distinct}, sorting can change which observations {cmd:in} selects
after the first variable. To compare results across commands, create a sample
indicator and select observations with {cmd:if}.

{pstd}With {opt sort()}, rows that tie on the sort key stay in the order of
{varlist}. {cmd:distinct} displays tied rows in an order that can change from
one run to the next.

{pstd}With no observations in memory, {cmd:cdistinct} does what both commands
do when {cmd:if} excludes every observation: it displays the note, or a table
of zeros with {cmd:minimum(0)}, and returns 0. {cmd:distinct} stops with error
r(111) in that case unless {opt joint} is specified.

{pstd}{cmd:distinct} leaves its table behind in Mata as the global matrices
{cmd:mout}, {cmd:ndistinct}, and {cmd:ntotal}. {cmd:cdistinct} creates no Mata
objects.

{pstd}The package of {cmd:distinct} also contains {cmd:distinctgen}, which
generates a variable holding the number of distinct values. ctools does not
reimplement it; use {help distinctgen} itself.


{* ctools-attribution:begin (generated by scripts/sync_attribution.py; edit config/attribution.json)}{...}
{marker acknowledgments}{...}
{title:Acknowledgments}

{pstd}
{cmd:cdistinct} reimplements {cmd:distinct}, written by Gary Longton (Fred
Hutchinson Cancer Research Center) and Nicholas J. Cox (Durham University).
Its syntax, options, output, and stored results follow {cmd:distinct}.

{pstd}
{cmd:cdistinct} is pinned to this version of {cmd:distinct}:

{p2colset 9 27 29 2}{...}
{p2col:Command}{cmd:distinct}{p_end}
{p2col:Authors}Gary Longton and Nicholas J. Cox{p_end}
{p2col:Version}1.4.3{p_end}
{p2col:Date}September 3, 2023{p_end}
{p2col:Header line}{cmd:*! 1.4.3 NJC 3 September 2023}{p_end}
{p2col:Source}{it:Stata Journal} 23(4), package dm0042_5
({cmd:net install dm0042_5, from(http://www.stata-journal.com/software/sj23-4)}),
distributed November 8, 2023. This is the date of the package file in the
{it:Stata Journal} software archive. The package does not state a distribution
date. SSC distributes an older version, 1.2.1 (March 1, 2012).{p_end}
{p2col:Latest version?}Yes, as of November 25, 2026.{p_end}
{p2colreset}{...}

{pstd}
SSC distributes an older version of {cmd:distinct}, 1.2.1 (March 1, 2012). The
authors have since published four versions in the {it:Stata Journal}: 1.3.0
(July 22, 2015; dm0042_2, 15(3)), 1.4.1 (October 10, 2020; dm0042_3, 20(4)),
1.4.2 (August 15, 2022; dm0042_4, 23(2)), and 1.4.3 (September 3, 2023;
dm0042_5, 23(4)). {cmd:cdistinct} follows 1.4.3. Compared with the SSC copy,
it displays a different table, uses different defaults for {cmd:abbrev()} and
{cmd:minimum()}, and accepts {cmd:sort()} and {cmd:width()}.
{cmd:search dm0042, entry} lists the {it:Stata Journal} versions. The same
package contains the companion command {cmd:distinctgen}. ctools does not
reimplement {cmd:distinctgen}.

{pstd}
{cmd:cdistinct} changes only when ctools is updated to a newer version of
{cmd:distinct} and tested against it. {cmd:which distinct} shows the version
installed on your machine. {help ctools##acknowledgments:help ctools} lists
the pinned versions of all ctools commands.

{pstd}
Please report bugs and questions about {cmd:cdistinct} at
{browse "https://github.com/mdroste/stata-ctools/issues":github.com/mdroste/stata-ctools/issues}.
The authors of {cmd:distinct} do not maintain {cmd:cdistinct}. Undocumented
differences between {cmd:cdistinct} and {cmd:distinct} are bugs in
{cmd:cdistinct}.

{pstd}
According to its help file, {cmd:distinct} grew out of a program that Patrick
Royston posted to Statalist.

{pstd}
If you use {cmd:cdistinct} in published work, please cite the original
command:

{phang2}Cox, N. J., and G. M. Longton. 2008. Speaking Stata: Distinct
observations. {it:Stata Journal} 8(4): 557-568.{p_end}

{phang2}Longton, G., and N. J. Cox. 2002. DISTINCT: Stata module to display
distinct values of variables. Statistical Software Components S424201, Boston
College Department of Economics. Revised March 21, 2012.{p_end}

